##Starting openvswitch ##
--------

Before starting ovs-vswitchd itself, you need to start its configuration
database, ovsdb-server. Each machine on which Open vSwitch is installed should
run its own copy of ovsdb-server. Before ovsdb-server itself can be started,
configure a database that it can use::

       sudo  mkdir -p /usr/local/etc/openvswitch
       sudo ovsdb-tool create /usr/local/etc/openvswitch/conf.db \
           vswitchd/vswitch.ovsschema


# Create a log file 
	cd /usr/local/var, sudo mkdir log , cd log,sudo mkdir openvswitch,cd openvswitch, sudo touch ovsdb-server.log
	
Configure ovsdb-server to use database created above, to listen on a Unix
domain socket, to connect to any managers specified in the database itself, and
to use the SSL configuration in the database::

    sudo mkdir -p /usr/local/var/run/openvswitch
    sudo  ovsdb-server --remote=punix:/usr/local/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock \
        --remote=db:Open_vSwitch,Open_vSwitch,manager_options \
        --private-key=db:Open_vSwitch,SSL,private_key \
        --certificate=db:Open_vSwitch,SSL,certificate \
        --bootstrap-ca-cert=db:Open_vSwitch,SSL,ca_cert \
        --pidfile --detach --log-file

.. note::
  If you built Open vSwitch without SSL support, then omit ``--private-key``,
  ``--certificate``, and ``--bootstrap-ca-cert``.)

Initialize the database using ovs-vsctl. This is only necessary on the first run
after you create the database with ovsdb-tool, though running it at any time is
harmless::

    sudo ovs-vsctl --no-wait init

Start the main Open vSwitch daemon, telling it to connect to the same Unix
domain socket::

    sudo  ovs-vswitchd --pidfile --detach --log-file

# Validating
 
	sudo ovs-vsctl --version
	sudo ovs-vsctl show


	 sudo ovs-vsctl add-br br0
     sudo ovs-vsctl add-port br0 eth0
	 
	 
!!!!! Register node with ODL for OVSDB. This is called passive mode in ODL!!!!

# ODL uses the opendaylight-iid value as the OVSDB node ID when a node registers with it. The opendaylight-iid key/value pair is available in the external-id column of the open_vswitch table on the node. 
# If opendaylight-iid key/value pair is not configured , odl uses uuid as the node id. We set this value to srv-03-01 to help with CRUD  operations. In case of active mode where ODL initiates the connection to the Open vSwitch  , it will
# automatically set opendaylight-iid key value so that it can use node id.

# Check to ensure that  opendaylight-iid is not set

sudo ovs-vsctl list open_vswitch

# Now restart  openvswitch by setting opendaylight-iid and system-id.  We set system-id to random , which generates a random number to uniquely id the openvswitch instance on the node.

 sudo /usr/local/share/openvswitch/scripts/ovs-ctl restart --system-id=random  \
 --external-id="opendaylight-iid=/network-topology:network-topology/network-topology:topology[network-topology:topology-id='ovsdb:1']/network-topology:node[network-topology:node-id='ovsdb:srv-04-01']"


#Verify

sudo ovs-vsctl list open_vswitch

# set the node  to connect to the odl for mgmt via OVSDB

	 sudo ovs-vsctl set-manager tcp:172.20.254.221:6640
	 
#Verify in ODL using REST that  the node is connected.

sudo ovs-vsctl show

# Create /etc/rc.local with 
# #!/bin/sh -e
# exit 0
# Make rc.local executable
chmod +x rc.local

#add the following to /etc/rc.local/ before exit 0 to ensure that openvswitch starts after reboot. 

 sudo /usr/local/share/openvswitch/scripts/ovs-ctl start --system-id=random  \
 --external-id="opendaylight-iid=/network-topology:network-topology/network-topology:topology[network-topology:topology-id='ovsdb:1']/network-topology:node[network-topology:node-id='ovsdb:srv-04-01']"

# Load openvswitch kernel module on boot by editing /etc/modules file
 
sudo nano /etc/modules
	openvswitch

# verification
lsmod | grep openvswitch

